================
Rogue DHCP Detection

Why we did this
================
DHCP Snooping protects the network by allowing DHCP replies only from trusted ports.

However, administrators still need visibility into who is attempting to act as a DHCP Server.

An attacker may connect:

- Personal Router
- WiFi Hotspot
- Home Broadband Router
- Unauthorized DHCP Server

These devices can start distributing incorrect IP addresses and disrupt the network.

Administrators therefore need a method to identify and detect unauthorized DHCP Servers before they impact users.

This led to Rogue DHCP Detection.

Rogue DHCP Detection helps identify devices attempting to provide DHCP services from unauthorized ports.

What will happen
================
1. DHCP Snooping monitors DHCP traffic.
2. User ports remain untrusted.
3. If a device sends DHCP Offer or DHCP ACK messages on an untrusted port:
   - The packet is dropped.
   - The event is logged.
   - The administrator can identify the source port.
4. The rogue DHCP device can be traced and removed.

Example

Legitimate DHCP Server
        |
        |
      G0/1
   (Trusted)

        Switch
      /       \
     /         \
 User PC     Rogue Router

Result:
--------
Legitimate DHCP Server:
Allowed

Rogue DHCP Server:
Detected and Blocked

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Identifies unauthorized DHCP Servers.
2. Protects users from incorrect IP configurations.
3. Prevents traffic redirection.
4. Enhances DHCP Snooping security.
5. Helps locate rogue devices quickly.
6. Improves network visibility.
7. Reduces troubleshooting time.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Requires DHCP Snooping.
2. Trusted ports must be configured correctly.
3. Does not stop DHCP Starvation attacks by itself.
4. Requires monitoring and log review.
5. Static IP devices are not tracked through DHCP bindings.

Hence what came next
--------------------
After securing DHCP services, attackers looked for another attack vector.

Instead of sending fake DHCP information, they started exhausting DHCP address pools by sending thousands of fake DHCP requests.

This attack is called:

DHCP Starvation Attack

The attack consumes all available IP addresses, preventing legitimate users from obtaining connectivity.

This led to:

DHCP Starvation Attack Demonstration

so administrators could understand how the attack works and how DHCP Snooping Rate Limiting mitigates it.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Enable DHCP Snooping

Switch(config)# ip dhcp snooping

Switch(config)# ip dhcp snooping vlan 10,20,30


Configure Trusted DHCP Server Port

Switch(config)# interface g0/1
Switch(config-if)# ip dhcp snooping trust


User Ports Remain Untrusted
(Default Behavior)

Switch(config)# interface range fa0/1-24
Switch(config-if-range)# no ip dhcp snooping trust


Verification
============

show ip dhcp snooping

show ip dhcp snooping binding

show ip dhcp snooping statistics

show logging

